From 491087f5d15c252fbeab0b41418f0db070d5e0f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 23:14:32 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Polycrystal: one material for all the grains

Option --single-material: the elements are given the attribute 1 before
the creation of the problem, the orientations of the grains are given
point by point (material axes as partial quadrature functions), and the
behaviour is integrated by one batch over all the grains instead of one
batch per grain. All the grains have the same material properties, only
their orientations differ. The averages of the stresses per grain are
written in the same file and format (avgStressPolycrystal), with one
reduction over the processes instead of two per grain. The grain of each
integration point, read by these averages, is kept whatever the symmetry
of the behaviour: only the orientations are specific to the orthotropic
behaviours.

On 4 MI300A (400 grains, 13 million unknowns), the time of a Newton
iteration falls from 0.93 s to 0.65 s: the work of the GPUs was split into
about 8,000 kernels per iteration, one per grain and per phase. On the CPU
(100 grains), the macroscopic stresses differ by 3e-13 relatively (other
order of the sums of the assembly).

Tests: UniaxialPolycrystalTest-SingleMaterial (axial stress of Cast3M),
UniaxialPolycrystalTest-SingleMaterial-Comparison and, on 2 processes,
UniaxialPolycrystalTestPar-SingleMaterial-Comparison. The comparisons run
the case with and without the option, each run in its own directory, then
compare the main output files and the averages per grain
(cmake/run-and-compare.py, CMake function mm_opera_hpc_add_compared_test).
The three tests expect the number of grains, which is written when the
option is taken into account.
